es (anonymous-staff-survey): 1) 'The new IVR dumps people in the wrong queue and they're cross before I've said hello.' 2) 'Half the April lot still transfer anything they don't know.' 3) 'Since the price rise every call is an argument.' 4) 'I used to just sort out a wrong bill. Now half of them go off to BRT and the customer rings back.' 5) 'No breathing space between calls any more, occupancy is through the roof.' 6) 'Team leaders only care about handle time.'\n- Complaints log, monthly summary, Customer Relations (system-record): Complaints per 10,000 contacts: Jan 40, Feb 42, Mar 41, Apr 47, May 50, Jun 49, Jul 50. The complaint category field is optional on the logging form, and 64% of complaints since January are uncategorised. Of categorised complaints for April–July, the largest category is 'Price/tariff' (31%), followed by 'Service – delay' (24%) and 'Billing accuracy' (19%). Complaints are counted by the month received, not linked to the originating call.\n- Notes of conversation with Lewis Abara, team leader for the April-intake nesting group, 6 August (manager-account): 'My twelve are hitting handle time and their QA is improving week by week. I can't tell you their FCR. The dashboard only gives the site number, and MI produce agent-level FCR only on request. Honestly, on the floor they don't look much different to my old hands, but that's my impression, not data. I'd be gutted if they went on performance plans for a site-wide number.'\n\nBRIEF:\nFCR fell because of the 14 April Finance change, not the new starters. That change cut the credit an agent can apply on a call from £100 to £25. Larger adjustments are now blocked and sent automatically to the back-office Billing Resolution team. Billing calls referred there rose from 7% to 54% in the week of 14 April. Referred calls resolve first time only about 33% of the time, against 82% for non-referred calls. Customers then call back to ask about their referral. The April intake is not the cause. Their ref